0,3,8,8,3],[0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The puzzle: **every solid rectangle gets hollowed out. Its one-cell border keeps its colour, and everything strictly inside the border becomes 8.**\n\n**What the grid contains.** Each input has a black (0) background and a few solid single-colour rectangles. Each output has the same rectangles. The only change is that some of their cells turn to 8.\n\n**Where the 8s go:**\n- **Pair 1.**\n  - The 4×4 red square at rows 1–4, cols 1–4 gets 8 at rows 2–3, cols 2–3. That is its inside with a one-cell margin.\n  - The 3×6 blue rectangle at rows 7–9, cols 2–7 gets 8 only in row 8, cols 3–6. Again this is the inside with a one-cell margin.\n- **Pair 2.**\n  - The 6×5 red rectangle gets 8 at rows 2–5, cols 3–5.\n  - The 3×3 blue square gets a single 8 in its centre.\n  - The 5×7 green rectangle gets 8 at rows 9–11, cols 9–13.\n  \n  Each is the bounding box shrunk by one cell on every side.\n- **Pair 3.** The green, red and blue rectangles follow the same pattern. The fact sheet lists every changed cell, and all of them lie inside a shrunken box.\n\nColour, size and position do not matter. Every rectangle is treated the same way. A rectangle that is only 1 or 2 cells thick would have no interior, but none occur here.\n\n**Applying it to the test:**\n- Blue 4×5 rectangle at rows 0–3, cols 1–5 (it touches the top edge): interior is rows 1–2, cols 2–4.\n- Blue 3×3 at rows 1–3, cols 8–10: its centre (2,9) becomes 8.\n- Red 6×6 at rows 5–10, cols 2–7: interior is rows 6–9, cols 3–6.\n- Green 7×4 at rows 7–13, cols 9–12: interior is rows 8–12, cols 10–11.\n\nThe program finds the objects, fills each interior with 8, and emits one teaching stage per rectangle.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    for o in objects(g, bg=bg):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        if y1 - y0 >= 2 and x1 - x0 >= 2:\n            out[y0 + 1:y1, x0 + 1:x1] = 8\n            emit(out, f\"Fill the interior of the colour-{o['colour']} rectangle \"\n                      f\"({y0},{x0})-({y1},{x1}) with 8, keeping its 1-cell border.\")\n    emit(out, \"All rectangles hollowed: borders kept, interiors filled with 8.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Blue 4×5 rectangle at rows 0–3, cols 1–5 (it touches the top edge): interior is rows 1–2, cols 2–4."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Red 6×6 at rows 5–10, cols 2–7: interior is rows 6–9, cols 3–6."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Green 7×4 at rows 7–13, cols 9–12: interior is rows 8–12, cols 10–11."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each is the bounding box shrunk by one cell on every side."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"96a859532b61e270b70b604a45cfc081a594b885fe8bd088400e57e96a70fe1a","task_sha256":"fd1ec440e091065e9d191122c34302cfcd9d0a481cc38044eebe08acaddcf9ad","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
